@page "/rally-x"

<PageTitle>Rally-X — Screen9</PageTitle>

<div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-4xl flex-1 flex-col gap-3 p-4">
    <h1 class="text-xl font-bold text-pc-white">Rally-X — C#</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-pc-gray">
        <code class="text-pc-yellow">samples/csharp/RallyX</code>'s <code class="text-pc-yellow">RallyXGame</code>, the same game that
        runs in a native pixel window, in mode 12 (640×480, 16 colours). Click the screen, then drive:
        arrows or WASD, <span class="text-pc-white">Space</span> for smoke, <span class="text-pc-white">P</span> to pause.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded border border-pc-yellow bg-pc-blue/30 p-3 text-sm">
        <div class="mb-1 font-bold text-pc-yellow">Work in progress</div>
        <p>
            Rally-X is not finished. What works: the maze, the red cars that chase you, flags, rocks, smoke, fuel, lives and rounds,
            and an autopilot that plays it. Still to do: tuning (speeds, chase behaviour and difficulty are not balanced against the
            arcade), mouse/touch control, a saved high score, proper sound effects, and a closer camera like the original's.
            The art is the original Rally-X (Namco/Midway), used here for demonstration only. It is the second game on the engine,
            after Gorillas — feedback is welcome, on <a href="https://github.com/marcoparenzan/Screen9/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-pc-brightcyan underline hover:text-pc-yellow">GitHub Issues</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <Screen9.Web.Client.Components.RallyXWasm @rendermode="new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false)" />
</div>
